<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Etna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aetna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Etna is an active volcano on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the highest mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, about 3,300 metres high. Its fertile lower slopes attracted Greek colonists, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catana</a> at its foot in 729 BCE, and its eruptions repeatedly damaged the city. Greek writers placed the forge of Hephaestus and the buried giant Typhon beneath it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-etna.jpg" alt="Mount Etna" width="320" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BenAveling · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mt_Etna_and_Catania1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
